  "record": {
    "read_status": "full",
    "file_problem": "Full paper and supplementary text are present.",
    "relation": "uses_without_test",
    "tranche": "T4",
    "tranche_reason": "Tests whether adding a different explicit regularizer changes held-out classification performance during CNN transfer learning; it does not test the listed version-specific interventions.",
    "gate_note": "Reports PtR results against fine-tuning baselines across datasets, architectures, and settings. Cited results from other methods are not recorded as this study's results.",
    "test_reason": "The study evaluates its own pseudo-task regularizer, PtR. It uses dropout and small batches as regularization choices but does not vary dropout, compare batch sizes under fixed epoch budgets, or test any other listed version. Random regression targets regenerated during training do not test memorization of fixed random labels.",
    "authors_conclusion": "The record broadly captures the authors' conclusion: PtR provides an efficient alternative to auxiliary regularizers requiring concrete objectives or extra resources, with reported accuracy gains often comparable to recent methods. Their gradient-noise and flatter-minima explanation is a proposed mechanism; loss-landscape measurements are explicitly left for future work. Claims of consistent gains should acknowledge the conflicting ResNet-101 MIT67 entries.",
    "recipe": "different",
    "recipe_differences": "Adds an on-line randomized pseudo-regression objective to pretrained CNN fine-tuning; evaluates sparse-data transfer-learning classification rather than random-label fitting, width, optimizer, batch-size, dropout, sharpness, or memorized-example removal.",
    "method_sensitivity": "Dataset, architecture, regression loss, weight decay, batch normalization, training from scratch versus ImageNet initialization, and timing threshold T are varied or discussed; gains differ across settings.",
    "fits_no_version": [
      {
        "finding": "PtR generally improves held-out accuracy over vanilla fine-tuning across the reported transfer-learning tasks. The ResNet-101 MIT67 result is inconsistent between the main and supplementary tables.",
        "proposed_claim": "An auxiliary regression objective with targets regenerated randomly during training can improve CNN transfer-learning accuracy without additional source-domain data.",
